The team maps domain errors in one global filter:

@Catch(DomainError)
export class DomainErrorFilter implements ExceptionFilter {
  catch(error: DomainError, host: ArgumentsHost) {
    const { status, error: name } = STATUS_OF(error);
    host.switchToHttp().getResponse<Response>()
      .status(status).json({ statusCode: status, error: name, message: error.message });
  }
}

// main.ts
app.useGlobalFilters(new DomainErrorFilter());

A course row with a null prerequisite list makes EnrollmentService.enroll throw:

TypeError: Cannot read properties of null (reading 'filter')

Statement: "Because DomainErrorFilter is registered globally, this TypeError is also handled by DomainErrorFilter and goes through its STATUS_OF table."

The rule "you cannot enroll in a draft course" lives in the controller:

@Post()
async enroll(@Body() dto: EnrollRequestDto, @CurrentUser() user: User) {
  const course = await this.courses.findById(dto.courseId);
  if (!course || !course.published) throw new NotFoundException();
  return this.enrollments.enroll(user.id, dto.courseId);
}

A nightly job in the same app imports a partner's CSV and calls EnrollmentService.enroll(userId, courseId) for every row. The next morning:

SELECT course_id, count(*) FROM enrollment WHERE enrolled_at >= '2026-10-23' GROUP BY 1;
 api-design-production | 214    <- a draft course

Statement: "If the published check had been inside EnrollmentService.enroll instead of the controller, the nightly job could not have enrolled learners in the draft course."
